## What is a function?

A **function** is a reusable piece of code that performs a task. Instead of writing the same logic repeatedly, you put it inside a function and call it whenever you need it.

For example, a task manager might need to calculate how many tasks are still open.

~~~js
function countRemaining(completed, total) {
  return total - completed;
}

const remaining = countRemaining(3, 10);
console.log(remaining); // 7
~~~

The function receives two **parameters**, `completed` and `total`, performs a calculation, and returns a value.

Functions are one of the most important building blocks in JavaScript. React components are functions, event handlers are functions, and many array operations such as `map()` and `filter()` receive functions.

## Function declarations

A **function declaration** uses the `function` keyword and gives the function a name.

~~~js
function greet(name) {
  return `Hello, ${name}!`;
}
~~~

You call the function by writing its name followed by parentheses.

~~~js
const message = greet("Anna");
~~~

Function declarations are especially useful for named operations that are central to your program.

One useful property of function declarations is **hoisting**. JavaScript makes function declarations available before their position in the source code.

~~~js
sayHello();

function sayHello() {
  console.log("Hello!");
}
~~~

Although this works, many developers still prefer putting declarations before their first use because it makes the code easier to read.

## Function expressions

A function can also be stored in a variable. This is called a **function expression**.

~~~js
const greet = function (name) {
  return `Hello, ${name}!`;
};
~~~

The function itself is an expression that produces a value, and that value is assigned to `greet`.

Unlike a function declaration, the function expression cannot be called before the variable has been initialized.

~~~js
const greet = function (name) {
  return `Hello, ${name}!`;
};

greet("Anna");
~~~

Function expressions are useful when you want to treat a function as data, such as storing it in a variable or passing it to another function.

## Arrow functions

Modern JavaScript provides a shorter syntax called an **arrow function**.

~~~js
const add = (a, b) => {
  return a + b;
};
~~~

When the function contains only one expression, you can use an **implicit return**. The expression's result is returned automatically.

~~~js
const add = (a, b) => a + b;
~~~

For one parameter, parentheses can be omitted.

~~~js
const double = number => number * 2;
~~~

For zero parameters, parentheses are required.

~~~js
const getMessage = () => "Hello!";
~~~

Arrow functions are particularly common for short callbacks and React code.

~~~js
const prices = [10, 20, 30];
const doubled = prices.map(price => price * 2);
~~~

A practical guideline is to use normal function declarations for clearly named standalone operations and arrow functions for short functions, callbacks, and functions assigned to variables. This is a convention rather than a strict language rule.

## Parameters and arguments

A function definition contains **parameters**. The actual values supplied when calling the function are called **arguments**.

~~~js
function createGreeting(name) {
  return `Hello, ${name}`;
}

createGreeting("David");
~~~

Here, `name` is the parameter and `"David"` is the argument.

A function can have several parameters.

~~~js
function calculateTotal(price, quantity) {
  return price * quantity;
}

calculateTotal(15, 3);
~~~

JavaScript does not require you to provide every argument. Missing parameters receive `undefined` unless you provide a default.

## Default parameters

A **default parameter** provides a fallback value when an argument is missing or `undefined`.

~~~js
function greet(name = "Guest") {
  return `Hello, ${name}!`;
}

greet();
// "Hello, Guest!"
~~~

This is useful for functions with optional configuration.

~~~js
function createTask(title, completed = false) {
  return {
    title,
    completed
  };
}
~~~

The caller can provide only the required information while the function supplies a sensible default.

## Rest parameters

Sometimes you want a function to accept any number of arguments. A **rest parameter** collects the remaining arguments into an array.

~~~js
function sum(...numbers) {
  return numbers.reduce((total, number) => total + number, 0);
}

sum(1, 2, 3, 4);
// 10
~~~

The `...numbers` syntax means that all supplied arguments after the normal parameters are collected into `numbers`.

You can combine regular and rest parameters.

~~~js
function logMessage(prefix, ...messages) {
  console.log(prefix, messages);
}

logMessage("INFO", "Started", "Connected", "Ready");
~~~

## Return values

A function can send a result back to its caller using `return`.

~~~js
function multiply(a, b) {
  return a * b;
}

const result = multiply(4, 5);
~~~

When JavaScript reaches `return`, the function finishes immediately.

~~~js
function isAdult(age) {
  if (age >= 18) {
    return true;
  }

  return false;
}
~~~

A function without a return value produces `undefined`.

~~~js
function logTask(title) {
  console.log(title);
}

const result = logTask("Buy milk");
// result is undefined
~~~

A useful habit is to make a function's purpose clear: either it calculates and returns something, performs an intentional side effect, or does both when there is a good reason.

## Functions are values

One of JavaScript's most important features is that functions are **first-class values**. This means you can store them in variables, put them into arrays or objects, and pass them to other functions.

~~~js
const sayHello = () => "Hello";
const action = sayHello;

console.log(action());
~~~

You can also store functions in an array.

~~~js
const operations = [
  () => 2 + 2,
  () => 10 * 3
];

console.log(operations[0]());
~~~

This idea is fundamental to callbacks.

## Callbacks

A **callback** is a function passed to another function so that the other function can call it later.

For example, `map()` receives a callback that describes how each array item should be transformed.

~~~js
const prices = [10, 20, 30];

const withTax = prices.map(price => price * 1.27);
~~~

The arrow function is a callback. `map()` calls it for each item.

Event handlers work the same way.

~~~js
button.addEventListener("click", () => {
  console.log("Button clicked");
});
~~~

You give the browser a function. The browser calls that function when the click happens.

React uses the same concept.

~~~jsx
function Button() {
  const handleClick = () => {
    console.log("Clicked");
  };

  return <button onClick={handleClick}>Save</button>;
}
~~~

The function is passed as a value to `onClick`.

## Scope

**Scope** describes where a variable can be accessed.

~~~diagram
{"type": "layers", "title": "Scope is a set of nested boxes", "caption": "Code looks up a name in its own box first, then in each outer box. Outer code can never look inside.", "layers": [{"label": "Global scope", "items": ["const TAX = 0.27"]}, {"label": "Function scope: total()", "items": ["let sum", "TAX ✓"]}, {"label": "Block scope: for { }", "items": ["const item", "sum ✓", "TAX ✓"], "highlight": true}]}
~~~

JavaScript has several kinds of scope, but two are particularly important here: function scope and block scope.

Variables declared with `let` and `const` are **block-scoped**. A block is code surrounded by curly braces, such as the body of an `if` statement.

~~~js
if (true) {
  const message = "Inside";
  console.log(message);
}

// message is not available here
~~~

The variable exists only inside the block.

Function declarations and variables declared inside a function are available within that function's scope.

~~~js
function createMessage() {
  const message = "Hello";
  return message;
}

console.log(createMessage());
// message itself is not available here
~~~

This separation prevents unrelated parts of your application from accidentally modifying local variables.

## Closures

A **closure** is one of the most useful and initially confusing concepts in JavaScript.

~~~diagram
{"type": "flow", "title": "A closure remembers", "nodes": [{"id": "mk", "label": "makeCounter()", "sub": "let count = 0", "kind": "code"}, {"id": "ret", "label": "returns increment", "sub": "() => ++count"}, {"id": "env", "label": "Captured scope", "sub": "count lives on", "highlight": true}, {"id": "c1", "label": "counter()", "sub": "→ 1, 2, 3 …", "kind": "code", "highlight": true}], "edges": [{"from": "mk", "to": "ret"}, {"from": "ret", "to": "env", "label": "keeps a link"}, {"from": "env", "to": "c1", "label": "each call"}]}
~~~

A closure happens when a function remembers variables from the surrounding scope even after that surrounding function has finished running.

Consider a counter.

~~~js
function createCounter() {
  let count = 0;

  return function () {
    count++;
    return count;
  };
}

const counter = createCounter();

counter(); // 1
counter(); // 2
counter(); // 3
~~~

`createCounter()` finishes, but the returned function still has access to `count`.

The variable is not exposed directly. The returned function controls how it changes.

This is a practical form of **encapsulation**, meaning that internal data is kept private and accessed through controlled operations.

### Independent closures

Each call to `createCounter()` creates a new scope.

~~~js
const first = createCounter();
const second = createCounter();

first();  // 1
first();  // 2
second(); // 1
~~~

The counters are independent because each returned function closes over a different `count` variable.

### Click handlers can remember state

Closures are also useful for browser event handlers.

~~~js
function createClickHandler() {
  let clicks = 0;

  return function () {
    clicks++;
    console.log(`Clicked ${clicks} times`);
  };
}

const handleClick = createClickHandler();

button.addEventListener("click", handleClick);
~~~

Every click calls the same function, and the function remembers the `clicks` variable.

You will encounter this idea repeatedly in frontend development. React state provides a more structured way to manage changing UI data, but understanding closures helps you understand what JavaScript is doing underneath.

## Pure functions and side effects

A **pure function** is a function that produces the same result for the same inputs and does not change anything outside itself.

~~~js
function add(a, b) {
  return a + b;
}
~~~

Calling `add(2, 3)` always produces `5`, and the function does not modify external state.

A **side effect** is an interaction with something outside the function's own calculation. Examples include changing the DOM, writing to a database, modifying an external variable, or logging to the console.

~~~js
function saveTask(task) {
  localStorage.setItem("task", task);
}
~~~

This function has a side effect because it changes browser storage.

Pure functions are often easier to test and reason about. Side effects are necessary in real applications, but it is useful to keep them deliberate and easy to identify.

For example, calculating a cart total can be pure:

~~~js
function calculateCartTotal(items) {
  return items.reduce((total, item) => total + item.price, 0);
}
~~~

Saving that total to an API would be a separate operation with a side effect.

## A short note about this

The `this` keyword refers to a value determined by how a function is called. Regular functions and arrow functions behave differently here.

A regular function can receive a `this` value based on the call site.

~~~js
const user = {
  name: "Anna",
  greet() {
    return `Hello, ${this.name}`;
  }
};

user.greet();
// "Hello, Anna"
~~~

Arrow functions do **not** create their own `this`. They capture `this` from the surrounding scope.

~~~js
const user = {
  name: "Anna",
  greet: () => `Hello, ${this.name}`
};
~~~

This is one reason arrow functions should not automatically replace regular methods in objects or classes. In React, arrow functions are common for callbacks, while `this` is much less central in modern function components.

## Common mistakes

### Calling a callback immediately

Suppose a function expects a callback:

~~~js
setTimeout(showMessage, 1000);
~~~

This passes the function itself.

This is different:

~~~js
setTimeout(showMessage(), 1000);
~~~

Here, `showMessage()` is called immediately and its return value is passed to `setTimeout()`.

### Forgetting return

~~~js
const double = number => {
  number * 2;
};
~~~

Because the arrow function uses curly braces, you need an explicit `return`.

~~~js
const double = number => {
  return number * 2;
};
~~~

Or use an implicit return:

~~~js
const double = number => number * 2;
~~~

### Accidentally sharing state

If mutable state is outside a function, multiple calls can unexpectedly affect each other.

Closures can solve this by creating private state inside each invocation.

### Modifying external data unnecessarily

A function that changes an external array can be harder to reason about than one that returns a new result.

~~~js
function addItem(items, item) {
  return [...items, item];
}
~~~

This style is particularly useful in React because React state updates are commonly expressed by producing new values instead of mutating existing state.

## Why this matters for React

React applications are built around functions.

A component is usually a function that receives input and returns UI.

~~~jsx
function Task({ title }) {
  return <li>{title}</li>;
}
~~~

Event handlers are functions. Array transformations use callbacks. React hooks depend heavily on JavaScript's function and closure behaviour.

For example, a task list might transform data into components:

~~~jsx
const tasks = [
  { id: 1, title: "Learn JavaScript" },
  { id: 2, title: "Build a React app" }
];

const elements = tasks.map(task => (
  <li key={task.id}>{task.title}</li>
));
~~~

Once you understand functions as values, callbacks, scope, and closures, code like this becomes much easier to read.

The next step is to start combining these ideas with arrays and objects, because real applications rarely work with isolated values. They work with collections of users, products, tasks, bookings, and API responses.

